&lt;h1 class="relative group">Opening a Power: Where the Coefficients Come From
&lt;div id="opening-a-power-where-the-coefficients-come-from" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#opening-a-power-where-the-coefficients-come-from" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>A power such as $(x+y)^{55}$ is 55 factors of $(x+y)$. Each factor contributes an $x$ or a $y$. A finished term is one pattern of those choices. The two powers in the term always add to 55. The number in front of the term counts how many patterns give that same pair of powers.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>&lt;a href="https://dasarpai.github.io/dsblog/equations-and-their-shapes">Functions, Equations, and Their Shapes&lt;/a> asks what a polynomial looks like as a graph. This article asks where the numbers in front of the terms come from.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>